// Grapes reference data — 27 grapes, each anchored to one or more regions.js
// regions via `regionIds`. Powers Regions.jsx's third toggle mode ("Grapes"),
// a different lens on the same 26-region dataset rather than a separate
// destination — see PROJECT_MEMORY.md §26.
//
// Schema locked in grapes_research_brief_tranche1.md Step 2, held unmodified
// across all 5 research tranches. Every field except `id`/`names`/`regionIds`/
// `confusedWith` is a { value, confidence } pair — confidence is one of:
//   'green'  — well-documented (3+ independent sources, or one authoritative/
//              peer-reviewed source)
//   'yellow' — documented, thinner sourcing (2 sources, or one credible source
//              with no contradiction found)
//   'red'    — contested/inconsistent; value states the honest gap directly
//              (e.g. "No exact number found") rather than an invented figure
//
// Confidence tags are internal data, never surfaced in the UI — same
// decision already made for regions.js's own sourcing (kept out of the app,
// not out of the codebase).
//
// `short` is a display-only field, present on body/tannin/acidity/colour/abv
// only (the 5 Structure-chip fields) — a hand-written 2-5 word value shown
// in the compact chip UI, so a long descriptive `value` (some run past 200
// characters) doesn't blow out the chip's fixed layout. Derived directly
// from each field's own `value` (the real core fact, sourcing commentary
// stripped), not invented — the full `value` text stays available behind
// a "Read more" toggle in the same chip, never lost. No other field has a
// `short` variant — Secondary/tertiary, Confused with, and Food pairing
// render as plain paragraphs with no fixed-height container to overflow,
// so they never needed one.
//
// String ids, matching regions.js's own convention (REGIONS uses id: 'bordeaux',
// not a number) — jumpTo()-style lookups in Regions.jsx already compare on
// string id, and a string slug is self-documenting inside an array literal
// (regionIds: ['bordeaux', 'napa']) without needing a separate numeric lookup
// table that could drift out of sync with the data it names.
//
// `names` is always an array, even for single-name grapes, so combined-name
// cases (Syrah/Shiraz, Primitivo/Zinfandel, Pinot Gris/Grigio,
// Ugni Blanc/Trebbiano) don't need a different shape — always display both
// names together per the standing decision.
//
// `regionIds` are real regions.js ids — every one of the 27 grapes below has
// at least one real anchor (the "some grapes have no regions.js anchor"
// premise floated during scoping did not hold up once checked against the
// real file; see PROJECT_MEMORY.md §28's own correction). Several grapes
// anchor more than one region and several regions anchor more than one
// grape — this is a genuine many-to-many relationship, not an edge case.
//
// `confusedWith` holds only grape ids that exist in this file. Several
// research entries name a taste-alike that isn't one of the 27 (Dolcetto,
// Gamay standalone, Verdejo, Pinot Blanc, Nebbiolo) — those are real, sourced
// facts but stay as plain text inside the `confusedWithNote` field rather
// than a broken/half-reciprocal link.
//
// Two known regions.js corrections surfaced by this research should be
// applied directly in regions.js itself, not duplicated here: Marlborough's
// Sauvignon Blanc share (~90% → ~80%) and Priorat's Grenache/Garnacha
// blend-partner phrasing. Tracked separately, not yet applied.
//
// `grapeType` is 'red' | 'white' | 'sparkling' — added for the Grapes UI's
// visual colour-coding (see the grapes toggle mockups). Classified by each
// grape's actual dominant real-world style, not mechanically from the
// tannin field alone — tannin correctly separates red from white, but
// can't tell a still white (Sauvignon Blanc) from a grape that's white-
// skinned but overwhelmingly made into sparkling wine (Glera). Two grapes
// are genuine multi-category cases, called out at their own entry rather
// than silently flattened: Pinot Meunier is classified 'sparkling' despite
// a real minority of still red/rosé production (Champagne blending is its
// dominant real-world role); Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains is classified
// 'white' despite spanning still/sparkling/fortified about as evenly as
// any grape in this file — its own styleRange field is the more honest
// source if this distinction ever matters more than the UI needs it to.

